examples.input:
Audit this IAM policy attached to our application's EC2 role:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "*",
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}

findings.remediation:
Replace with a scoped policy that grants only the specific permissions the application needs:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "S3AppBucket",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject",
        "s3:ListBucket"
      ],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::my-app-bucket",
        "arn:aws:s3:::my-app-bucket/*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Sid": "SQSQueues",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "sqs:SendMessage",
        "sqs:ReceiveMessage",
        "sqs:DeleteMessage"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:sqs:us-east-1:123456789012:my-app-queue"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "CloudWatchLogs",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "logs:CreateLogStream",
        "logs:PutLogEvents"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:logs:us-east-1:123456789012:log-group:/app/*"
    }
  ]
}

Process: 1. Enable CloudTrail and IAM Access Analyzer. 2. Run Access Analyzer policy generation to discover actually-used permissions. 3. Create a scoped policy based on actual usage. 4. Test in staging. 5. Apply to production. 6. Monitor for access denied errors for 2 weeks.

examples.input:
Audit this Terraform security group:

resource "aws_security_group" "app" {
  name = "app-sg"
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id

  ingress {
    from_port   = 22
    to_port     = 22
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  ingress {
    from_port   = 443
    to_port     = 443
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  ingress {
    from_port   = 3306
    to_port     = 3306
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["10.0.0.0/8"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

findings.remediation:
Option A (preferred): Remove SSH ingress entirely. Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager for shell access — no open ports needed:

ingress {
  # REMOVED: port 22 from 0.0.0.0/0
}

Option B: Restrict to VPN CIDR or specific IPs:

ingress {
  from_port   = 22
  to_port     = 22
  protocol    = "tcp"
  cidr_blocks = [var.vpn_cidr]  # e.g., "10.100.0.0/24"
  description = "SSH from VPN only"
}
